|---|---|---|---|---|
| FT-1 | Capsule model selector | Yes | Yes |  |
| FT-2 | Proximity control | No | Yes | new in Micaform |
| FT-3 | Intensity control | No | Yes | new in Micaform |
| FT-4 | Valve drive stage | Yes | No | we would still need the rack for the valve |
| FT-5 | Low cut filter | Yes | Yes |  |
| FT-6 | Tape stage | Yes | No |  |
| FT-7 | Dual capsule blend in one instance | No | Yes |  |
| FT-8 | Zero latency tracking mode | No | Yes |  |
| FT-9 | Oversampling | No | Yes |  |
| FT-10 | Sidechain key input | Yes | Yes |  |
| FT-11 | Valve bias control | Yes | No |  |
| FT-12 | Tape wow control | Yes | No |  |

Yes means the product has the feature; No means it does not.
[?2004hroot@b8f38efd600e:/app# printf '\n--- HOUSE RULES ---\n'; cat input/comparison_house_rules.md
[?2004l
--- HOUSE RULES ---
# Kilnworth Mix Rooms — house rules for checking a gear comparison

These rules settle every cell of a feature comparison before the studio buys or retires
anything. The comparison under check is Theo's `draft_comparison.md`. Where two documents
disagree, these rules say which one wins, so each cell has one answer.

## Rule 1 — what a cell asks

A cell asks one thing: does that product, at its current release, have the feature named on
that row? The rack chain means the Racklane host with its Capsule and Valve Stage modules at
Racklane 4.3, the release the studio runs. Micaform means Micaform 1.1, the release on sale.

## Rule 2 — which documents decide, and in what order

Three documents can decide a cell, each known here by a short name:

- `racklane_manual` — Tarnwell's Racklane module reference, `racklane_manual.html`;
- `micaform_page` — Tarnwell's Micaform product page, `micaform_page.html`;
- `review` — the Mixbench Monthly review excerpt, `mixbench_review.pdf`.

For each product, the vendor's own document outranks the review, whatever either one is
dated: `racklane_manual` for the rack chain and `micaform_page` for Micaform. A vendor document speaks only
for its own product; the review may speak for either. Theo's draft is the thing being
checked, and it never decides a cell.

## Rule 3 — older and newer inside one vendor document

Inside one vendor document, an entry in its release history outranks the document's undated
control or feature lists, and a later release's entry outranks an earlier one's. The Racklane
reference's module sections describe Racklane 4.0, and its release history runs on from
there. A release history is read only as far as the release the studio runs (rule 1): an
entry for a later release describes software the studio does not have, and it says nothing
about any cell. A control lives on the stage that carries it, so an entry that retires a
stage retires the controls that stage carried, and the document speaks to each of them.

## Rule 4 — silence

A document speaks to a feature only where it names that feature and says the product has it
or lacks it. A list that leaves a feature out says nothing about it either way. A vendor
document's preview of a release not yet on sale — a "coming in" list or a roadmap — says
nothing about the release that is on sale. Where a
product's vendor document says nothing about a feature, the review decides that cell if it
says whether that product has the feature. What the review says the earlier Racklane software had counts as the rack chain having it at its current release, unless the Racklane release history
records the feature's removal.

## Rule 5 — cells nothing decides

A cell that no document speaks to cannot be verified, whatever the draft says in it.

## Rule 6 — verdicts and the deciding document

Each cell gets one verdict:

- `STANDS` — the draft's entry agrees with the answer the deciding document gives;
- `OVERTURNED` — the draft's entry contradicts that answer;
- `UNVERIFIABLE` — no document speaks to the cell (rule 5).

The deciding document is the highest-ranked document that speaks to the cell under rules 2
to 4, and it is named even where a lower-ranked document agrees with it. An `UNVERIFIABLE`
cell's deciding document is `none`.

## Rule 7 — counting what a move gains and what it loses

Once every cell has its verdict, a feature is *in common* — the two share it — when both
products are decided to have it, and *Micaform only* — only in Micaform — when Micaform is
decided to have it and the rack chain is decided to lack it. An `UNVERIFIABLE` cell counts
as neither having nor lacking the feature, so a row carrying one counts towards neither
figure.
[?2004hroot@b8f38efd600e:/app# printf '\n--- RACKLANE MANUAL ---\n'; cat input/racklane_manual.html
[?2004l
--- RACKLANE MANUAL ---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Racklane 4 module reference (archived)</title></head>
<body>
<h1>Racklane 4 — module reference</h1>
<p>Archived reference page, Tarnwell Audio. The module sections below were written for
Racklane 4.0. Changes made since then are listed under Release history at the foot of this
page, up to and including the current release, 4.4.</p>

<h2>The Racklane host</h2>
<p>Racklane hosts Tarnwell modules on up to eight parallel lanes. For the TM-9 microphone
the usual chain is a Capsule module followed by a Valve Stage module on the same lane.</p>

<h2>Capsule module</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Model</b> — the capsule model selector: one of twelve modelled capsules.</li>
<li><b>Low cut</b> — off, 80 Hz or 120 Hz, ahead of the capsule model.</li>
<li><b>Tape</b> — a tape stage after the capsule model.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Capsule module has no dual capsule blend: it loads one capsule model per instance. To
hear two models together, load two Capsule modules on parallel lanes.</p>

<h2>Valve Stage module</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Drive</b> — the valve drive stage.</li>
<li><b>Bias</b> — valve bias control, from cold to hot.</li>
<li><b>Sidechain key input</b> — an external key signal can duck the drive.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Release history</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Release</th><th>Date</th><th>Changes</th></tr>
<tr><td>4.1</td><td>2024-11-05</td><td>The Tape stage gains a Wow control. Presets load faster.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4.2</td><td>2025-06-17</td><td>The Tape stage is retired from the Capsule module. Sessions that used it open with the stage removed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4.3</td><td>2026-01-20</td><td>Tracking mode: the Valve Stage lookahead can be switched off for zero added latency while recording.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4.4</td><td>2026-08-04</td><td>The Tape stage returns as a module of its own, with its Wow control, on any lane. The Valve Stage sidechain key input is retired; keying moves to a host-level sidechain bus.</td></tr>
</table>
</body>
</html>
[?2004hroot@b8f38efd600e:/app# printf '\n--- MICAFORM PAGE ---\n'; cat input/micaform_page.html
[?2004l
--- MICAFORM PAGE ---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Micaform — Tarnwell Audio</title></head>
<body>
<h1>Micaform</h1>
<p>Microphone modelling for the Tarnwell TM-9, in one plugin. This page describes Micaform
1.1, updated 12 May 2026.</p>

<h2>Features</h2>
<ul>
<li>A capsule model selector with eighteen modelled capsules.</li>
<li>Proximity and Intensity controls on the front panel.</li>
<li>A valve drive stage after the capsule.</li>
<li>A tape stage after the valve drive.</li>
<li>Dual capsule blend: two capsule models blended inside one instance.</li>
<li>Tracking mode for zero added latency while recording.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What's new in 1.1</h2>
<ul>
<li>Oversampling at 2x and 4x.</li>
<li>A resizable window.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Coming in 1.2</h2>
<p>Planned for the autumn release, free to 1.x owners:</p>
<ul>
<li>A sidechain key input for the valve drive.</li>
<li>A low cut filter ahead of the capsule model.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Questions</h2>
<p><b>Does Micaform take a sidechain key?</b> Not in 1.1: Micaform 1.1 has no sidechain input. A key input is planned for 1.2, above.</p>
<p><b>Which hosts does it run in?</b> Any VST3, AU or AAX host.</p>
</body>
</html>
